<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Orange-Nassau</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Orange-Nassau is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> royal family, descended from the German house of Nassau, whose member <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-silent">William the Silent</a> inherited the Principality of Orange in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1544. As stadtholders its princes led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, and William III also became King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Since 1815 the family has provided the monarchs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-orange-nassau.jpg" alt="House of Orange-Nassau" width="320" height="348" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johannes Evert van Leeuwen (1855-1931) SVG is Own work · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Koninklijk_wapen_van_het_koninkrijk_der_Nederlanden.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-nassau">House of Nassau</a> began with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nassau-castle">Nassau Castle</a>, founded around 1100 by Dudo, Count of Laurenburg. In 1255 the Nassau possessions were divided between Walram and Otto, sons of Count Henry II. Walram's line became Dukes of Nassau and, in 1290, Grand Dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>. Otto's line inherited properties in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. The marriage of Henry III of Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breda">Breda</a> and Claudia of Chalon-Orange in 1515 led to the family's claim to Orange: their son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-of-chalon">René of Chalon</a> inherited the Principality of Orange in 1530, and after his death in 1544 his cousin William of Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dillenburg">Dillenburg</a> inherited his lands and became the founder of the house.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>William of Orange stood up for the Protestant, mainly Calvinist, inhabitants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> against the persecution begun under Philip II. The Eighty Years' War started in 1568, and the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brielle">Brielle</a> by rebels in 1572 turned its fortunes, though William was assassinated in 1584 by a hired killer sent by Philip. His sons Maurice and Frederick Henry succeeded him, and Frederick Henry's son William II died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> in 1650, leaving a posthumous son, William III. The regents then left the stadtholdership vacant in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-stadtholderless-period">First Stadtholderless Period</a>. William III later became King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><p>When William III died childless in 1702 the principality was disputed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-william-friso">John William Friso of Nassau-Dietz</a> and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-i-of-prussia">Frederick I of Prussia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-utrecht">Treaty of Utrecht</a> ceded Orange to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1713. After the French invasion of 1747 William IV was made stadtholder in several provinces, and the office became hereditary in all of them. William V fled the French in 1795. After the French were driven out in 1813, his son William Frederick, as William VI, became King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> in 1815.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelmina-of-the-netherlands">Queen Wilhelmina</a> reigned for 58 years, from 1890 to 1948, and became a symbol of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> resistance in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. The house died out in the male line with her, but it continues in the female line, and the name Orange is still used by the royal family. Since the Royal House Membership Act 2002, the royal house is a smaller group than the wider royal family. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-silent">William the Silent</a> was buried in the New Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delft">Delft</a>, where most of the house, including all Dutch monarchs, has been buried since.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Orange-Nassau” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The princes of <b>Orange-Nassau</b> held the provincial office of stadtholder in most provinces and the command of the army, which gave them a quasi-monarchical standing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Luxembourg was therefore a personal possession of the <b>House of Orange-Nassau</b> rather than part of the Kingdom of the Netherlands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchy of Luxembourg · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom itself had been created in 1815, when the Congress of Vienna united the former Dutch Republic, which the French had turned into the Batavian Republic and then the Kingdom of Holland, with the former Austrian Netherlands under William I of the <b>House of Orange-Nassau</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The States appointed him, and nearly always chose a prince of the <b>House of Orange-Nassau</b>, beginning with William the Silent, so that the office became effectively hereditary.</blockquote><a href="/societies/dutch-republic">Social Hierarchy of the Dutch Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>Lingen then changed hands between Spain and the house of Orange during the Eighty Years' War, and the <b>Oranges</b> founded a Calvinist academy there in 1697.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-lingen">County of Lingen</a></li><li><blockquote>Walram's line produced King Adolf of Nassau, elected in 1292, and later the grand dukes of Luxembourg; Otto's line produced William the Silent and the house of <b>Orange-Nassau</b>, today's Dutch royal family.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-nassau">County of Nassau</a></li></ul></section>
